{
"slug": "journal-wifi-dead-zones", "cat": "Networking", "date": "2026-09-15",
"title": "Why your Wi-Fi drops out in the rooms you use most",
"img": "rack", "alt": "Network patch cables plugged into a rack-mounted switch",
"excerpt": "Your provider's router was never meant to cover a whole house. What interferes with the signal, and the fixes that work, in the order we try them.",
"body": """
<p>Once internet reaches your house, it should simply be everywhere in it. In practice most homes run on the router the cable company supplied, and most homes have a room where video stalls, calls break up and the smart speaker loses its connection. People tend to live with it. You do not have to.</p>
<h2>What gets in the way</h2>
<p>Wi-Fi is radio, and radio is affected by what it passes through. Dense building materials are the biggest factor: concrete, plaster and metal all weaken the signal, and so does glass. Then there is everything else competing for the same airwaves.</p>
<ul>
<li>Appliances near the router, such as a refrigerator or water heater</li>
<li>Other wireless devices, including garage door openers</li>
<li>Distance from the router to the rooms you use most</li>
<li>Neighboring networks, especially in apartments and townhouses</li>
</ul>
<h2>Start with the router</h2>
<p>If yours has been in place for years, replacing it is the first step. Look for a current Wi-Fi standard and at least dual-band support, so devices can use 2.4 GHz or 5 GHz as appropriate. Band steering helps further by moving each device to the better band automatically.</p>
<p>Location matters as much as hardware. A router in a corner of the basement, beside the cable entry, broadcasts mostly into the foundation. Put it centrally where you can. That can be difficult depending on the layout and the wiring in the walls, which is one reason to call someone who runs cable for a living.</p>
<p>If the router is current and well placed, check its channel. A free analyzer app will show how crowded nearby channels are, and you can change yours in the router's settings.</p>
<blockquote>Extenders are a patch. Wired access points are a plan.</blockquote>
<h2>Then extend the network properly</h2>
<p>Wi-Fi extenders are easy: plug one in where the signal is still good and it repeats the network further. They often cut the available speed, and they add another device to manage.</p>
<p>Access points are the better answer for larger homes. Each one connects back to the router by Ethernet and broadcasts a strong signal of its own. Most are powered over the same cable through a PoE switch or injector, so there is no outlet to find. Several access points placed with intent give full coverage and full speed throughout the house.</p>
<p>Depending on your home, you might need one of these steps or all of them. If you would rather we look at it, a site visit will show exactly where the gaps are and why.</p>
"""},
{
"slug": "journal-security-panel", "cat": "Security", "date": "2026-08-20",
"title": "What your security panel can do that you are not using",
"img": "facade-detail", "alt": "Warm light behind a dark timber facade at night",
"excerpt": "Chime mode, user codes, text alerts and phone control. Most homeowners use a fraction of what their alarm panel already offers.",
"body": """
<p>The control panel is the brain of a security system. Ours are typically Honeywell and Resideo panels, installed in a metal cabinet in the basement or utility room with a rechargeable backup battery that keeps the system running through a power cut. Most people arm it, disarm it, and never touch anything else. That leaves a lot on the table.</p>
<h2>Chime mode</h2>
<p>With chime turned on, every keypad sounds when a protected door opens, even while the alarm is off. It is a small feature with a real track record. One of our customers heard a chime at 5 AM, heard nothing else, and went back to sleep. A burglar had opened their basement door, heard the chime, and left. Several neighboring homes were broken into that morning.</p>
<h2>Individual user codes</h2>
<p>Give each person their own code: family members, a housekeeper, a dog walker. The panel records who armed and disarmed the system and when, and a code can be removed in seconds without changing anyone else's.</p>
<h2>Notifications that matter</h2>
<p>A connected panel can text you when the system is armed or disarmed, when a door opens, or when a fire, flood or carbon monoxide sensor trips. It can tell you the power has failed at the house and again when it comes back, which matters if you are away in winter.</p>
<blockquote>A panel that tells you nothing is only doing half its job.</blockquote>
<h2>Control from your phone</h2>
<p>Every residential security and fire system we install can be controlled from a smartphone: arm and disarm, check status and see the event history. Paired with a smart lock, it also lets you let someone in and lock up again from wherever you are.</p>
<h2>A reliable connection</h2>
<p>All of this depends on the panel being able to talk to the outside world. We connect our systems through a cellular radio rather than a phone or internet line, and the radio reports in with a daily test so a fault is caught quickly.</p>
<p>If your panel is a few years old, many of these features may already be there and simply switched off. A service visit can turn them on.</p>
"""},
{
"slug": "journal-hardwired-vs-wifi-cameras", "cat": "Surveillance", "date": "2026-07-22",
"title": "Hardwired or Wi-Fi cameras: how to choose",
"img": "cameras", "alt": "Rows of white security cameras mounted on a dark wall",
"excerpt": "Wi-Fi cameras are easy to buy and easy to regret. Where each type works, and why we wire cameras whenever we can.",
"body": """
<p>There are hundreds of camera models on the market, and many of them are built poorly or wear out quickly. A lot of them look excellent on day one. The difference shows up over years, and in how the system was designed and installed.</p>
<h2>Hardwired cameras</h2>
<p>Hardwired systems use network IP cameras cabled back to a network video recorder, or NVR. The NVR stores the footage on a large hard drive and connects to your network so you can watch live or review recordings from your phone, at home or away. Cameras can record continuously or on motion.</p>
<p>Because each camera has its own cable, video is constant and the picture does not degrade when the Wi-Fi is busy. Hardwired cameras also come in more shapes: cylindrical bullets, domes, and turrets, whose flat face gives cleaner infrared images at night. Most of the cameras we install see in the dark and record at 2 to 5 megapixels at home, with 4K available. Outdoor models have aluminum bodies rated for harsh weather.</p>
<h2>Wi-Fi cameras</h2>
<p>Wireless cameras are simpler to place, which is their main appeal. They still need power, and they need a strong Wi-Fi signal at the spot where they are mounted. Many record only part of each minute, so they are limited in what they capture.</p>
<p>They work well indoors, for example to tell you someone has come into the house. Outdoors they are less suited to alerts, because every change in light and every passing branch can trigger an event. Dozens of false notifications a day will drain your phone and your patience.</p>
<blockquote>Cameras need to be designed, not just bought.</blockquote>
<h2>Installation is half the result</h2>
<p>We recently visited a new customer whose previous installer had mounted outdoor cameras on brackets made for indoor use. They were rusting and staining the house. Knowing the products, and where each one can and cannot go, is part of the job.</p>
<p>Many homeowners start with a DIY kit and find the marketing promised more than it delivers. If you are deciding, we are happy to walk the property and show you where wiring is practical and where wireless makes sense.</p>
"""},
{
"slug": "journal-cellular-communicators", "cat": "Security", "date": "2026-06-10",
"title": "Why every system we install talks over cellular",
"img": "white-house", "alt": "A white, minimal house under a clear sky",
"excerpt": "Copper phone lines are disappearing and internet phone service is inconsistent. How a cellular radio keeps an alarm connected when it counts.",
"body": """
<p>An alarm is only as good as its ability to tell someone. When a sensor trips, the panel has to reach the monitoring station so a call can be made and help dispatched. For decades that happened over a copper phone line. That option is going away.</p>
<h2>The problem with phone and internet lines</h2>
<p>Copper phone service is being phased out and is no longer available in many areas. Its replacement, voice over IP from cable and fiber providers, was not designed to carry alarm signals and can be inconsistent when it works at all. Internet connections go down for maintenance, during storms, and whenever the router is unplugged.</p>
<p>There is also the obvious weakness: a wire on the outside of a building can be cut.</p>
<h2>How a cellular radio works</h2>
<p>Every system we install includes a cellular radio, a small module connected to the main panel. It sends alarm events by cellular signal to the monitoring station and to you. It also carries the connection that lets you control the system and receive notifications on your phone.</p>
<ul>
<li>There are no lines at the building to cut</li>
<li>It works when the internet is down</li>
<li>It sends a daily test, so we know quickly if something is wrong</li>
<li>It runs on the panel's backup battery during a power failure</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>The signal that matters most should not depend on your router.</blockquote>
<h2>What it means in practice</h2>
<p>A carbon monoxide sensor in a Pittstown home tripped at 1 AM. Our monitoring station reached the homeowner while the fire company was dispatched, and firefighters measured carbon monoxide at life-threatening levels. That chain of events depends on a signal getting through at the worst possible moment.</p>
<p>If your system still relies on a phone line, upgrading the communicator is one of the most worthwhile changes you can make. It is usually a single visit.</p>
"""},
{
"slug": "journal-smart-locks", "cat": "Access", "date": "2026-05-06",
"title": "Smart locks and the leak you fix from three states away",
"img": "smart-lock", "alt": "A hand holding a phone beside a smart lock on a door",
"excerpt": "Remote entry is the feature you forget about until you need it. Real scenarios, the two types of lock, and what happens when the batteries die.",
"body": """
<p>Imagine your alarm system tells you there is a water leak at home, and you are hours away. Someone needs to get in now. With a smart lock, you open the door from your phone for the plumber, then lock it again when they leave.</p>
<p>Or the quieter version: you want to know when the kids get home from school. The lock and the alarm can tell you they are in, and that the door is locked behind them.</p>
<h2>Two physical types</h2>
<p>Smart locks come as deadbolts or as handle and lever sets, in finishes such as oil rubbed bronze, satin nickel and bright brass. We install Yale, August, Schlage and Kwikset among others. Each has a Z-Wave or Zigbee radio inside, so it can talk to a control system and to your phone.</p>
<p>A smart deadbolt has a touchscreen or a button keypad for a four to six digit code, and a motor that drives the bolt. A lever lock works the same way with a handle. If a door has both, we make one of them the smart lock and keep the other as a manual lock for extra security.</p>
<blockquote>Every unlock is logged. Every code can be different.</blockquote>
<h2>Know the limits</h2>
<p>Remote control needs an internet connection at the house. If the internet is down, you cannot unlock from afar, though the keypad at the door still works.</p>
<p>And batteries do run out. The locks we use can still be opened when that happens: most have a hidden terminal underneath where a battery can be held to power the keypad long enough to enter your code.</p>
<h2>Bring in the garage</h2>
<p>Most garage door openers now have their own app that shows whether the door is open or closed. Some can only be controlled from the maker's app. Others work with platforms such as Control4 or Honeywell, so the garage, the locks and the alarm live together in one place. We know which is which, and will specify equipment that fits together, or keep it separate if you prefer.</p>
"""},
{
"slug": "journal-camera-licensing", "cat": "Commercial", "date": "2026-04-02",
"title": "NVR or server: the hidden cost of camera licenses",
"img": "server", "alt": "Server racks with bundled network cabling",
"excerpt": "Server-based camera systems can carry per-camera license fees that add up to thousands at each renewal. What to ask before you buy.",
"body": """
<p>Choosing a commercial camera system is usually framed as a question of cameras: how many, what resolution, which brand. The recording platform behind them matters as much, and it is where long-term costs hide.</p>
<h2>Network video recorders</h2>
<p>An NVR is an embedded recorder with its own operating system, camera inputs and hard drive storage. NVRs are cost effective, simple to manage and easy to use. They suit sites with up to about 32 cameras, which covers most offices, practices, retail stores and smaller schools.</p>
<h2>Server-based systems</h2>
<p>Larger sites use camera management software running on a Windows server built to specification. These systems handle an effectively unlimited number of cameras, and they need more maintenance. For these installs we use Panasonic's Video Insight.</p>
<p>The catch is licensing. Most server-based platforms charge a license fee for each camera, renewed every few years. Across dozens of cameras, that can mean thousands of dollars at every renewal, on top of hardware and service.</p>
<blockquote>Ask what the system costs in year five, not just on day one.</blockquote>
<p>There are alternatives. Panasonic also makes Advidia cameras, which come with free software and no license fees. On the right project that changes the total cost considerably.</p>
<h2>Where the recorder goes</h2>
<p>Whatever the platform, install the recorder somewhere inconspicuous, where it cannot easily be tampered with or removed. Footage is only useful if it survives the incident.</p>
<h2>Questions worth asking</h2>
<ul>
<li>How many cameras do we need now, and in five years?</li>
<li>Are there per-camera license fees, and how often do they renew?</li>
<li>Can several sites be viewed under one account?</li>
<li>Does the system link to access control, so door events carry a photo?</li>
</ul>
<p>We are happy to review an existing quote or system and tell you what it will cost to own, not only what it costs to buy.</p>
"""},
{
"slug": "journal-home-theater-planning", "cat": "Theater & Audio", "date": "2026-03-04",
"title": "Projector or 85-inch panel? Planning a home theater",
"img": "projector", "alt": "A projector beam crossing a dark room",
"excerpt": "Very large flat panels have changed the math on dedicated theaters. How we decide between a projector and a panel, and where the sound comes in.",
"body": """
<p>Most homes have one main place to watch: a family room, a living room or a den. Some have a dedicated theater. Both deserve a system designed for the room rather than bought off a shelf.</p>
<h2>The dedicated theater</h2>
<p>A traditional theater has a large screen, a high definition projector on the ceiling, a surround receiver and amplifier, and a set of speakers that can be visible or hidden. The equipment is often located elsewhere, in a closet or a rack room, so it does not take space or attention.</p>
<p>Projectors still deliver the largest image. They also need upkeep, including periodic lamp replacement on many models, and they want a room where light can be controlled.</p>
<h2>The case for a panel</h2>
<p>As prices on 80 to 90 inch panels have come down, many of our clients now choose one instead of a projector and screen. Total cost of ownership is usually lower, there is less maintenance, and a panel copes far better with daylight. In many rooms it is simply the better answer.</p>
<blockquote>Pick the display for the room, not the brochure.</blockquote>
<h2>Sound for everyday rooms</h2>
<p>Family rooms increasingly use a soundbar mounted above or below the TV. Good ones from Sonos, Denon HEOS, Bose and Polk sound excellent, and several can add wireless rear speakers and a subwoofer later. They also stream music, so the TV area becomes a music room too.</p>
<h2>Brands and a long relationship</h2>
<p>We work with Samsung, Sony, LG, Epson, Integra, Onkyo, Denon, Niles, Triad and MartinLogan, among others, and choose by room and budget. When you call us with a question years later, you speak to someone who knows how your system was put in and which parts are worth upgrading.</p>
"""},
